<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad Nami</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1873">1873</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Damad Ahmad Nami Bey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad Nami was a Syrian politician of Circassian origin, a son-in-law of the Ottoman sultan Abdul Hamid II, who served as head of the French-mandated State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 1926 to 1928. Appointed by the French during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-syrian-revolt">Great Syrian Revolt</a>, he headed a government that failed to win nationalist support and was replaced before the 1928 Constituent Assembly elections.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-nami.jpg" alt="Ahmad Nami" width="293" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sammy.aw · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmad_nami.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahmad Nami was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> in 1873 into a wealthy family related to the Ottoman dynasty. His father, Fakhri Bey, was governor of Beirut under Ottoman rule. Nami studied at the Ottoman Military Academy and received military training in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He married Ayşe Sultan, a daughter of Sultan Abdul Hamid II, which made him a damat, a son-in-law of the imperial house. After the Young Turks overthrew the sultan the family went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Nami returned to Beirut in 1918 and ran his family's businesses. His first language was Turkish, and he could hardly speak Arabic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The French entrusted Nami with forming a government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> with limited presidential powers, and on 28 April 1926 he formed his cabinet, with Husni al-Barazi as Minister of the Interior, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fari">Faris</a> al-Khury as Minister of Education and Lutfi al-Haffar as Minister of Commerce. In June 1926 these ministers resigned in protest at French policy towards the nationalist movement and were arrested by the French High Commissioner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, Henry de Jouvenel. Nami tried to secure their release, was himself threatened with prison, and replaced them with three pro-French politicians.</p><p>Nami opposed the creation of an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and argued for a wider regional framework to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> united. He sought a national army and entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, and he demanded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> compensate citizens whose homes had been destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-syrian-revolt">Great Syrian Revolt</a> of 1925-27 and declare a general amnesty so that Syrian exiles could return. The government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> objected and accused him of seeking to establish a monarchy, and he was removed from office on 15 February 1928.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1932 the French considered creating a throne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> with Nami as king, but the plan came to nothing, and in 1940 he was thought of as a candidate for the presidency until the National Bloc objected. In the 1940s he withdrew from public life and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, travelling at times to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to lecture on history and politics at the Sorbonne. He died on 13 December 1962. He and Ayşe Sultan had two sons, Omer and Osman, and a daughter who died in infancy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahmad Nami” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>French high commissioners appointed governments led by <b>Ahmad Nami</b> and later Taj al-Din al-Hasani.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-syria">State of Syria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Beirut, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. Died in Beirut, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.89,35.51&amp;year=1930" aria-label="Beirut on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5455 935 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ahmad Nami"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5905 1205h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5905 1205h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.89,35.51&amp;year=1930">What was here in 1930? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
